From eea07eeb9cac0593f4fc14303d0e32ba73de848b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:44:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extensions: tell an extension the window it opened closed

windows.create answered with the one id every window call reports, so
the page an extension opened had no id of its own, and the series
raised none of the tabs and windows events it declares. Bitwarden ends
a passkey request, and lets the site fall back, when its prompt's
window is removed. A reader who closed the prompt waited out the
site's timeout instead.

A page an extension opens with windows.create is now a window of its
own, holding that one tab, under an id no other window of the session
has. windows.create answers with it. windows.get answers for it while
the page is open, and fails with "No window with id" after. getAll
lists it after the browser's window, and it carries the type the
extension asked for. Asked from inside the page, getCurrent,
getLastFocused, tabs.getCurrent, and tabs.query with currentWindow or
lastFocusedWindow all answer that window. The engine does not know
which window the reader last focused, so the last focused window is
the one the call came from, as the current window is.

tabs.query reports every window's tabs under their own window id,
extension pages in a window of their own included, so an extension
finds its prompt by address and removes it, as Bitwarden does. The
browser's window lists its tabs less those pages, and its active tab
is never one of them.

When the page's WebContents goes, however it went, every extension
listening hears tabs.onRemoved for its tab, with isWindowClosing set,
and then windows.onRemoved for the window, as Chrome raises them. A
stopped worker is woken to hear them. A listener that filters by
window type hears about the window only if it asked for that type,
and hears it once. The page can close because the application deleted
it, because it called window.close(), or because the extension called
windows.remove or tabs.remove, which ask the page to close the way
window.close() does.

windows.remove refuses the browser's own window, and tabs.remove
refuses a tab that is not a window of its own. The reader's tabs and
the browser's window stay the application's to close, and no other
tabs or windows event is raised.

windows.create registers its first page as soon as it opens and holds
it weakly, because opening a further page runs the application, which
may close the first; the call then fails rather than answering with a
page that is gone. OpenPage likewise notices an application that lets
the page go while it is handed over.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
